1
00:00:00,862 --> 00:00:03,099
Most people use AI on their documents like

2
00:00:03,099 --> 00:00:05,312
this: upload the files, then ask

3
00:00:05,312 --> 00:00:08,724
questions. It works. But

4
00:00:08,724 --> 00:00:09,782
there is a problem.

5
00:00:11,153 --> 00:00:13,553
Every time you ask, the AI has to read the

6
00:00:13,553 --> 00:00:15,628
material from scratch and piece the answer

7
00:00:15,628 --> 00:00:18,403
together again. Whatever you learned from

8
00:00:18,403 --> 00:00:20,648
your last question, it doesn't remember.

9
00:00:21,391 --> 00:00:23,598
What you have read never becomes an asset.

10
00:00:24,341 --> 00:00:25,666
It's just a pile of files

11
00:00:25,666 --> 00:00:26,761
sitting in a folder.

12
00:00:28,141 --> 00:00:29,828
In this episode we try a different

13
00:00:29,828 --> 00:00:33,161
approach. We let the AI build you a wiki.

14
00:00:33,903 --> 00:00:36,003
It reads the sources itself, writes the

15
00:00:36,003 --> 00:00:38,466
pages itself, and flags the places where

16
00:00:38,466 --> 00:00:40,311
new material contradicts old.

17
00:00:41,053 --> 00:00:43,041
Your job is to find the sources and ask

18
00:00:43,041 --> 00:00:46,116
the questions. And one more thing, the

19
00:00:46,116 --> 00:00:47,423
most important of all:

20
00:00:47,491 --> 00:00:48,566
to call a stop when it

21
00:00:48,566 --> 00:00:49,736
gets something wrong.

22
00:00:51,104 --> 00:00:52,967
What follows is the record of one real

23
00:00:52,967 --> 00:00:55,999
working session. Twelve steps in all,

24
00:00:56,029 --> 00:00:58,199
including the places where things failed.

25
00:00:59,572 --> 00:01:02,710
Step one. Open a new session and choose a

26
00:01:02,710 --> 00:01:05,942
folder. No prompt is typed in this step,

27
00:01:05,947 --> 00:01:08,155
but it decides everything that follows.

28
00:01:08,897 --> 00:01:11,092
This folder is the AI's territory.

29
00:01:11,835 --> 00:01:13,197
Every file it can read or

30
00:01:13,197 --> 00:01:14,530
write lives inside it.

31
00:01:15,912 --> 00:01:18,844
Step two. Hand it the article.

32
00:01:19,587 --> 00:01:21,612
It was written by Andrej Karpathy, and

33
00:01:21,612 --> 00:01:23,144
it's called LLM Wiki.

34
00:01:23,887 --> 00:01:25,919
The prompt is about as short as it gets:

35
00:01:25,949 --> 00:01:26,837
three words and a

36
00:01:26,837 --> 00:01:29,032
link. Take a look.

37
00:01:30,403 --> 00:01:31,711
Now watch what it did.

38
00:01:32,453 --> 00:01:34,653
It went and fetched the web page, and what

39
00:01:34,653 --> 00:01:36,136
came back was a summary.

40
00:01:36,878 --> 00:01:38,816
On its own, it judged that the summary

41
00:01:38,816 --> 00:01:41,578
might be distorted. So it switched to a

42
00:01:41,578 --> 00:01:44,016
different URL, fetched again, and got the

43
00:01:44,016 --> 00:01:46,453
complete original. This kind of

44
00:01:46,453 --> 00:01:48,786
self-correction, you never asked for.

45
00:01:50,151 --> 00:01:52,001
The heart of the article is a three-layer

46
00:01:52,001 --> 00:01:54,776
architecture. At the bottom are the raw

47
00:01:54,776 --> 00:01:57,651
sources: the papers, articles and reports

48
00:01:57,651 --> 00:01:58,933
you collect yourself.

49
00:01:59,676 --> 00:02:01,233
This layer is read-only.

50
00:02:01,976 --> 00:02:04,471
The AI may look, but never modify.

51
00:02:05,213 --> 00:02:07,101
In the middle is the wiki, written and

52
00:02:07,101 --> 00:02:09,133
maintained entirely by the AI.

53
00:02:09,876 --> 00:02:12,601
You only read it. At the top is the

54
00:02:12,601 --> 00:02:15,088
rulebook, which tells the AI how this wiki

55
00:02:15,088 --> 00:02:17,176
is organized, and what to do when new

56
00:02:17,176 --> 00:02:18,471
material comes in.

57
00:02:19,858 --> 00:02:21,871
Karpathy's own way of working is to keep

58
00:02:21,871 --> 00:02:24,246
the AI open on one side and Obsidian on

59
00:02:24,246 --> 00:02:26,246
the other, watching in real time as it

60
00:02:26,246 --> 00:02:29,403
edits. His analogy is precise.

61
00:02:30,146 --> 00:02:32,358
Obsidian is the editor, the AI is the

62
00:02:32,358 --> 00:02:34,591
engineer, and the wiki is the code.

63
00:02:35,958 --> 00:02:39,233
Step four. Ask it to turn the article into

64
00:02:39,233 --> 00:02:42,096
teaching material. Something interesting

65
00:02:42,096 --> 00:02:44,933
happened here. It declined to translate

66
00:02:44,933 --> 00:02:46,878
the article sentence by sentence.

67
00:02:47,620 --> 00:02:49,833
Its reason: a one-to-one translation of

68
00:02:49,833 --> 00:02:51,770
the whole piece is just copying someone

69
00:02:51,770 --> 00:02:54,658
else's work. But it didn't stop and wait

70
00:02:54,658 --> 00:02:57,883
for you to answer. It gave its reason, and

71
00:02:57,883 --> 00:02:59,270
then delivered what it thought was

72
00:02:59,270 --> 00:03:00,328
the better version.

73
00:03:01,706 --> 00:03:04,018
After delivering the material, it proposed

74
00:03:04,018 --> 00:03:05,551
the next step on its own.

75
00:03:06,293 --> 00:03:07,906
It said we should make a template for the

76
00:03:07,906 --> 00:03:10,143
rulebook first, because what students most

77
00:03:10,143 --> 00:03:11,676
want to know after reading is:

78
00:03:11,768 --> 00:03:13,701
so what do I paste to the AI now?

79
00:03:15,069 --> 00:03:16,907
At this point you could simply tell it to

80
00:03:16,907 --> 00:03:19,482
go ahead. But let's ask a different

81
00:03:19,482 --> 00:03:22,307
question instead. What are you planning to

82
00:03:22,307 --> 00:03:24,827
do? This habit matters.

83
00:03:25,569 --> 00:03:27,644
For any task that will generate a lot of

84
00:03:27,644 --> 00:03:30,082
follow-on work, have the AI state its plan

85
00:03:30,082 --> 00:03:33,027
first. Changing a plan is cheap.

86
00:03:33,769 --> 00:03:36,127
Changing a finished product is expensive.

87
00:03:37,505 --> 00:03:39,787
Its answer: short is what works.

88
00:03:40,530 --> 00:03:42,305
The most common beginner mistake is

89
00:03:42,305 --> 00:03:44,367
turning the rulebook into a long, long

90
00:03:44,367 --> 00:03:47,812
specification. Then everybody loses.

91
00:03:48,555 --> 00:03:50,492
You can't finish reading it, so you don't

92
00:03:50,492 --> 00:03:53,592
dare change it. The AI reads it, but the

93
00:03:53,592 --> 00:03:55,180
key points get diluted, and

94
00:03:55,180 --> 00:03:56,600
it obeys even less.

95
00:03:57,972 --> 00:04:00,305
So what is this rulebook, exactly?

96
00:04:01,047 --> 00:04:03,230
The file is called Claude.md.

97
00:04:03,972 --> 00:04:06,297
It's just a plain text file, kept inside

98
00:04:06,297 --> 00:04:09,360
the project folder. The only special thing

99
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:10,430
about it is the name:

100
00:04:10,522 --> 00:04:12,672
the AI reads it automatically every time

101
00:04:12,672 --> 00:04:15,680
it starts. It offered an analogy.

102
00:04:16,422 --> 00:04:18,660
Think of the AI as an employee who arrives

103
00:04:18,660 --> 00:04:20,417
at work every day with amnesia.

104
00:04:21,160 --> 00:04:23,310
Without the rulebook, you have to explain

105
00:04:23,310 --> 00:04:25,767
the rules again in every new conversation,

106
00:04:25,922 --> 00:04:27,217
until you want to give up.

107
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:29,822
With the rulebook, the rules are written

108
00:04:29,822 --> 00:04:31,835
on the wall, and the employee sees them

109
00:04:31,835 --> 00:04:33,180
the moment they walk in.

110
00:04:34,544 --> 00:04:37,414
Step seven. Give it real data.

111
00:04:38,156 --> 00:04:40,464
Here we tell it the field is neuroscience,

112
00:04:40,556 --> 00:04:42,081
and give it the path to a folder of

113
00:04:42,081 --> 00:04:45,031
papers. The first thing it did was run a

114
00:04:45,031 --> 00:04:46,881
command to see what was actually in that

115
00:04:46,881 --> 00:04:49,889
folder. No designing in a vacuum.

116
00:04:50,631 --> 00:04:51,851
That's a good habit.

117
00:04:53,236 --> 00:04:54,773
Then it drafted an outline for the

118
00:04:54,773 --> 00:04:57,811
rulebook. Eight subdirectories, four

119
00:04:57,811 --> 00:05:00,593
domain-specific hard rules, nine sections.

120
00:05:01,336 --> 00:05:03,573
From data formats to workflows, very

121
00:05:03,573 --> 00:05:06,556
thorough. It looked very professional.

122
00:05:07,943 --> 00:05:10,113
But this plan had a fundamental problem.

123
00:05:11,491 --> 00:05:13,336
It had looked at six filenames.

124
00:05:14,078 --> 00:05:17,361
Six. It had not read a single paper.

125
00:05:18,103 --> 00:05:20,041
And from those six filenames it derived

126
00:05:20,041 --> 00:05:22,478
eight subdirectories, four hard rules, and

127
00:05:22,478 --> 00:05:26,016
nine sections. Worse, all four hard rules

128
00:05:26,016 --> 00:05:27,748
were bound to one narrow domain:

129
00:05:27,803 --> 00:05:29,661
fruit flies and connectomes.

130
00:05:31,046 --> 00:05:32,554
So the user said this.

131
00:05:33,296 --> 00:05:35,166
You've written this far too rigidly.

132
00:05:35,909 --> 00:05:37,821
This knowledge base isn't limited to this

133
00:05:37,821 --> 00:05:40,329
field. Scrap the plan.

134
00:05:41,071 --> 00:05:42,716
We'll fix things as we go.

135
00:05:43,459 --> 00:05:44,979
Don't write the rulebook yet.

136
00:05:45,721 --> 00:05:47,109
When a rule comes up during the

137
00:05:47,109 --> 00:05:48,441
work, add it then.

138
00:05:49,810 --> 00:05:51,680
The AI's response was clean.

139
00:05:52,422 --> 00:05:54,610
It admitted: deriving a whole set of hard

140
00:05:54,610 --> 00:05:56,922
rules from six filenames is writing laws

141
00:05:56,922 --> 00:05:58,747
for a knowledge base that doesn't exist

142
00:05:58,747 --> 00:06:00,760
yet. Then it scrapped the

143
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:03,317
plan. No defending it.

144
00:06:04,686 --> 00:06:07,373
Next, it scanned the folder next door, and

145
00:06:07,373 --> 00:06:09,323
found another batch of papers, with

146
00:06:09,323 --> 00:06:11,856
electron-microscopy image segmentation,

147
00:06:11,973 --> 00:06:13,923
and light-microscopy connectomics in

148
00:06:13,923 --> 00:06:16,611
mammals. In other words, that

149
00:06:16,611 --> 00:06:18,373
fruit-fly-only rulebook would have hit a

150
00:06:18,373 --> 00:06:21,448
wall within a week. The user's correction

151
00:06:21,448 --> 00:06:22,706
turned out to be right.

152
00:06:24,073 --> 00:06:25,623
If you remember one thing from this

153
00:06:25,623 --> 00:06:27,556
episode, remember this part.

154
00:06:28,298 --> 00:06:29,986
Rules should grow out of the friction of

155
00:06:29,986 --> 00:06:32,556
real work, not be legislated up front.

156
00:06:33,298 --> 00:06:35,618
Those two earlier steps looked productive:

157
00:06:35,673 --> 00:06:37,836
two versions designed, nine sections

158
00:06:37,836 --> 00:06:39,893
outlined, four hard rules set.

159
00:06:40,636 --> 00:06:42,543
But all of it rested on guesswork.

160
00:06:43,286 --> 00:06:45,723
That one word, scrap, saved dozens of

161
00:06:45,723 --> 00:06:47,181
pages of rework later.

162
00:06:47,923 --> 00:06:50,218
Overruling the AI is not a failure.

163
00:06:50,961 --> 00:06:53,143
It is how this method is supposed to work.

164
00:06:54,525 --> 00:06:56,682
There is no new prompt in the next part,

165
00:06:56,737 --> 00:06:58,707
but it's the part most worth watching,

166
00:06:58,850 --> 00:07:01,062
because it shows exactly what the AI does

167
00:07:01,062 --> 00:07:02,457
when it hits an obstacle.

168
00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:05,870
First, it tried to read the paper's PDF.

169
00:07:06,612 --> 00:07:09,525
It failed. The environment was missing a

170
00:07:09,525 --> 00:07:10,870
required component.

171
00:07:12,256 --> 00:07:14,289
It did not report failure and stop.

172
00:07:15,031 --> 00:07:16,789
It checked the environment first:

173
00:07:16,856 --> 00:07:18,619
the Python version, the installed

174
00:07:18,619 --> 00:07:21,481
packages. Not one of them could handle

175
00:07:21,481 --> 00:07:24,969
PDFs. So it chose a library and installed

176
00:07:24,969 --> 00:07:27,994
it. Its reason: one package that can

177
00:07:27,994 --> 00:07:30,344
extract both text and images, which it

178
00:07:30,344 --> 00:07:31,289
would need later.

179
00:07:32,676 --> 00:07:34,651
With the library installed, it tried to

180
00:07:34,651 --> 00:07:36,296
print the PDF's text.

181
00:07:37,038 --> 00:07:40,051
It failed again. This time, a Windows

182
00:07:40,051 --> 00:07:42,126
encoding problem: special characters

183
00:07:42,126 --> 00:07:44,613
couldn't be printed. So it changed

184
00:07:44,613 --> 00:07:47,458
strategy. Don't print directly.

185
00:07:48,201 --> 00:07:50,746
Write to a file, then read the file back.

186
00:07:52,112 --> 00:07:54,187
For that, it wrote a small program of its

187
00:07:54,187 --> 00:07:57,194
own. What the program does is simple:

188
00:07:57,349 --> 00:07:59,399
write the text of every page of the PDF

189
00:07:59,399 --> 00:08:02,474
into a file. That gets around the encoding

190
00:08:02,474 --> 00:08:04,744
limit. And it worked.

191
00:08:05,487 --> 00:08:06,832
Forty pages read.

192
00:08:08,211 --> 00:08:10,344
Finally, it wrote the first note.

193
00:08:11,086 --> 00:08:12,594
And it did one more thing.

194
00:08:13,336 --> 00:08:15,399
It recorded the three places it got stuck

195
00:08:15,399 --> 00:08:17,949
in the work log: couldn't read PDFs, the

196
00:08:17,949 --> 00:08:20,199
encoding problem, and text extraction

197
00:08:20,199 --> 00:08:21,956
garbling the plus-minus sign.

198
00:08:22,699 --> 00:08:24,211
Those records later became the

199
00:08:24,211 --> 00:08:25,394
source of the rules.

200
00:08:26,759 --> 00:08:28,571
Through all of this, you said not one

201
00:08:28,571 --> 00:08:31,559
word. This is the difference between an AI

202
00:08:31,559 --> 00:08:33,516
agent and an ordinary chatbot.

203
00:08:34,259 --> 00:08:36,434
It tries, it fails, it checks the

204
00:08:36,434 --> 00:08:38,834
environment, it installs software, it

205
00:08:38,834 --> 00:08:40,354
writes its own programs.

206
00:08:41,730 --> 00:08:43,650
Let's look at the result in Obsidian.

207
00:08:44,393 --> 00:08:46,230
The settings at the top of the file have

208
00:08:46,230 --> 00:08:48,188
become a clickable properties panel.

209
00:08:48,930 --> 00:08:50,613
The tables render properly.

210
00:08:51,355 --> 00:08:53,130
And the links in double square brackets

211
00:08:53,130 --> 00:08:55,130
show up even when the target page doesn't

212
00:08:55,130 --> 00:08:57,043
exist yet. That

213
00:08:57,043 --> 00:08:58,475
means: to be written.

214
00:08:59,846 --> 00:09:01,996
After reviewing the first version, we give

215
00:09:01,996 --> 00:09:03,428
revision instructions.

216
00:09:04,171 --> 00:09:06,091
Five of them: the full title;

217
00:09:06,183 --> 00:09:08,016
the abstract with a translation;

218
00:09:08,108 --> 00:09:09,846
a two-thousand-character article for

219
00:09:09,846 --> 00:09:11,121
high-school readers; a

220
00:09:11,121 --> 00:09:12,833
three-thousand-character article for

221
00:09:12,833 --> 00:09:15,196
graduate students; and the paper's figures

222
00:09:15,196 --> 00:09:17,641
inserted, with translated captions.

223
00:09:19,018 --> 00:09:21,193
To extract the figures, it wrote a second

224
00:09:21,193 --> 00:09:24,530
program. And once extracted, it actually

225
00:09:24,530 --> 00:09:26,738
opened each image to check its content,

226
00:09:26,868 --> 00:09:28,900
rather than guessing from page numbers.

227
00:09:29,643 --> 00:09:31,755
Eight images, matching figures one to

228
00:09:31,755 --> 00:09:32,763
eight of the paper.

229
00:09:34,133 --> 00:09:35,641
This is the second version.

230
00:09:36,383 --> 00:09:38,696
The same note now has the full title, the

231
00:09:38,696 --> 00:09:41,008
abstract with translation, a twenty-five

232
00:09:41,008 --> 00:09:43,071
hundred character general article, a

233
00:09:43,071 --> 00:09:45,096
sixty-seven hundred character technical

234
00:09:45,096 --> 00:09:47,028
article, and eight figures.

235
00:09:48,409 --> 00:09:51,446
Here is a key point. The more specific the

236
00:09:51,446 --> 00:09:53,929
instruction, the more usable the output.

237
00:09:54,671 --> 00:09:56,716
Make it better: that does nothing.

238
00:09:57,459 --> 00:09:59,434
Two thousand characters, high-school

239
00:09:59,434 --> 00:10:01,179
level, few adjectives:

240
00:10:01,234 --> 00:10:03,459
that works. Because every

241
00:10:03,459 --> 00:10:04,954
condition can be checked.

242
00:10:06,332 --> 00:10:08,382
In the whole process, you did only four

243
00:10:08,382 --> 00:10:11,632
things. Set the direction, provide real

244
00:10:11,632 --> 00:10:14,270
data, ask what a term means, and overrule

245
00:10:14,270 --> 00:10:17,245
a wrong design. That note, you didn't

246
00:10:17,245 --> 00:10:18,590
write a single word of it.

247
00:10:19,960 --> 00:10:21,960
Next episode, we take the friction

248
00:10:21,960 --> 00:10:24,285
accumulated in this one, and turn it into

249
00:10:24,285 --> 00:10:27,210
a real rulebook. Every rule written then

250
00:10:27,210 --> 00:10:28,872
will have a concrete failure as its

251
00:10:28,872 --> 00:10:31,505
reason. See you next time.
